From 04fe735b6c6213ba55ee89933c16a75259c2d231 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:45:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Compress to Target Size: name the limit, not the quality

The compressor asks "what quality?", which nobody knows the answer to.
Every exam board, visa portal, job site and government form instead
states a ceiling in kilobytes, so people were nudging a quality slider
and re-reading the size until it went under. This inverts it: name the
limit, get the best-looking encode that fits.

Quality is binary-searched rather than stepped, so a 12MP photo costs
about seven encodes instead of ninety, measured through bitmapBytes,
which counts the encoded stream without buffering it. If even quality 5
overshoots, the scale needed is computed from how far over it landed -
file size tracks pixel count, so sqrt(target/actual) lands close on the
first try instead of shrinking by a fixed step and encoding at each stop.
Shrinking is opt-in and off the critical path when quality alone suffices.

PNG is deliberately absent from the format row: it ignores the quality
argument, so there would be nothing for the search to turn. Failure is a
real answer here - if the target cannot be met the tool says so and says
which control would change that, rather than silently returning the
smallest thing it managed.

133 tools now, and every one has a pitch entry. No file under ads/ is
touched.

